  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 211 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\n19Z water vapor imagery continued to show the longer wavelength \ntrough over the Pacific Northwest. One shortwave had lifter into \nnorthern MO and southern IA with a second wave lifting towards the \nMT/Canada boundary. Surface obs showed a lee trough along the central\nRockies with southerly low level flow through the southern plains. \n\nThe forecast continues to have occasional chances for light rain \nthrough Thursday. The RAP/NAM/GFS continue to show a good warm air \nadvection pattern setting up tonight with isentropic surfaces \nindicating good lift with nearly saturated parcels. As a result the \nCAMs continue to develop isolated showers by the late evening and \ninto the early hours of Wednesday morning. Have continued with some \nslight chance POPs expecting the coverage of storms to remain \nsomewhat isolated. Then a weak frontal boundary is progged to move \ninto northeast and north central KS Wednesday night. There doesn't \nlook to be a lot of synoptic scale forcing for precip, but models \nhave been consistent in precip along the boundary as it moves into \nnortheast KS. In both cases, mid level lapse rates are progged to be \naround 6 C/km or weaker so there may be some rumbles of thunder, but \nstronger storms are not anticipated. \n\nA better defined shortwave is still on track to impact the forecast \narea Thursday night and Friday. Although the 12Z GFS has complicated \nthings with a much slower progression to the wave and some potential \nfor precip through the day Saturday. There is some increased spread \nin the ensembles with the handling of this shortwave energy. For \nexample the ECMWF keeps a progressive wave exiting the area Saturday \nmorning only to bring another vortmax through northeast KS Saturday \nnight. So confidence in the deterministic runs is a little below \nnormal until the shortwave details become more apparent. Again with \nthis wave, the GFS projects some rather shallow lapse rates ahead of \nthe wave and limits instability for strong convection. \n\nThe operational solutions seem to have a similar solution with upper \nlevel energy closing off and digging into the central plains before \ncutting off from the mean westerlies, and the spreads from the 00Z \nensembles had come a little closer. Still there are differences in \nthe strength of the upper low and where it cuts off. This may affect \nsome of the details of the sensible weather, but confidence in \nperiods of rain is improving. The 12Z runs seem to trend a little \nfurther south of the upper low and keeping the surface low mainly \nsouth of the forecast area. This would make it difficult for the \nwarm sector and better instability to advect into the forecast area. \nBut it bares watching as the wind profiles could support organized \nupdrafts and a risk for severe weather. \n\nTemperatures through the end of the work week look to remain above \nnormal so long as there is reasonable insolation. A strong pressure \ngradient and deep mixing should help push highs to near 80 on \nWednesday. Increasing clouds on Thursday and an easterly component \nto the low level winds may keep highs in the middle 70s. Then clouds \nand precip chances for Friday and Saturday look to cause highs to \ntrend cooler. More seasonal temperatures are forecast by the end of \nthe forecast period as the potential for some cold air advection \nincreases behind the low pressure system.",
